## Limits & assumptions

These are the things this analysis does **not** model. Read them before spending money
on the strength of the numbers above.

- **Greedy self-consumption only.** The battery charges from PV surplus and discharges
  to cover household deficit. It never buys cheap grid energy to use later, so if your
  tariff has a wide day/night spread, a smarter battery could beat these figures.
- **No battery degradation.** Capacity is held constant for the battery's whole life.
  Real cells fade, typically to 70–80% over a warranty period, so long paybacks here
  are optimistic — the later years save less than the first.
- **No energy price inflation.** Today's tariff is applied to every future year. Rising
  electricity prices would shorten the payback; falling prices would lengthen it.
- **Naive payback.** Battery cost divided by year-1 savings. No discounting, no cost of
  capital, no financing, no incentives, subsidies or tax deductions.
{% if r.resolution_minutes < 60 %}
- **Hourly netting.** Your data is {{ r.resolution_minutes }}-minute; it is summed to hourly before simulating, so
  surplus and deficit inside the same hour cancel out. This slightly *understates* what
  a real battery would do — a conservative direction.
{% else %}
- **Hourly netting.** Data is analysed hourly, so surplus and deficit inside the same
  hour cancel out. This slightly *understates* what a real battery would do — a
  conservative direction.
{% endif %}
{% if r.seasonality_warning %}
- **Period analysed: {{ r.days_analyzed }} days** ({{ r.period_start[:10] }} to {{ r.period_end[:10] }}). This is **less than a
  full year**, so seasonality is not fully captured: annualized figures extrapolate the
  seasons present in the data to the ones that are not, and PV output between a summer
  month and a winter month can differ by a factor of four. The annual numbers above
  could be wrong in either direction by considerably more than the differences between
  the scenarios.
{% else %}
- **Period analysed: {{ r.days_analyzed }} days** ({{ r.period_start[:10] }} to {{ r.period_end[:10] }}). That is a full year, so
  seasonal swings in both PV and consumption are captured rather than extrapolated.
{% endif %}
- **Battery specification is assumed, not measured.** Charge/discharge power limits,
  round-trip efficiency and minimum state of charge come from the values passed on the
  command line. A real installation's inverter limits and standby losses will differ.
